From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?John=20T=C3=B6rnblom?= <john.tornblom@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:15:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] PS4: do not ask the kernel for the working directory

The game resolves its search paths with realpath(), which (for a
relative path) calls getcwd(), which is the kernel's __getcwd,
syscall 326. The PS4's kernel has that syscall; the PS5's kernel, on
which a PS4 title runs, does not: its system call table has no slot
326, and a call to an absent slot kills the process with SIGSYS. The
game data's folder is "." by default, so the game died there, at
startup, before any window: signal 12 in libkernel's system call
stub, rdi the 1 KiB buffer of realpath(), rsi PATH_MAX.

The PS4 cannot rely on the working directory anyway (see 0004: a file
is not found by a relative path in the application's sandbox), so the
game data's folder is the client's folder, and the savegame's files
have absolute names. That leaves getcwd() no work to do: the default
game data folder is the client's folder, a relative path is resolved
against the client's folder instead of the working directory, and the
working directory is neither read nor changed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01EVpxQnEDpmH9ifdSUZDpqm
---
 src/backends/unix/system.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/common/filesystem.c    |  8 ++++++++
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/backends/unix/system.c b/src/backends/unix/system.c
index d28e83b..0085dbf 100644
--- a/src/backends/unix/system.c
+++ b/src/backends/unix/system.c
@@ -748,6 +748,16 @@ Sys_RemoveDir(const char *path)
 qboolean
 Sys_Realpath(const char *in, char *out, size_t size)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// realpath() calls getcwd() for a relative path, and the PS5's kernel
+	// has no __getcwd: the process dies with SIGSYS. Nothing here is a
+	// symlink, so a relative path is resolved against the client's folder.
+	if (in[0] != '/')
+	{
+		snprintf(out, size, "%s%s", Sys_GetBinaryDir(), in);
+		return true;
+	}
+#endif
 	char *converted = realpath(in, NULL);

 	if (converted == NULL)
@@ -840,6 +850,14 @@ Sys_LoadLibrary(const char *path, const char *sym, void **handle)
 void
 Sys_GetWorkDir(char *buffer, size_t len)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// getcwd() is syscall 326, and the PS5's kernel has no such slot:
+	// the process dies with SIGSYS. Nothing reads the working directory
+	// there: the savegame's files have absolute names (see 0004), and
+	// the working directory is not changed either.
+	buffer[0] = '\0';
+	return;
+#endif
 	if (getcwd(buffer, len) != 0)
 	{
 		return;
@@ -851,6 +869,12 @@ Sys_GetWorkDir(char *buffer, size_t len)
 qboolean
 Sys_SetWorkDir(char *path)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// A file is not found by a path relative to the working directory in
+	// the application's sandbox (see 0004), so the working directory is
+	// left where it is.
+	return true;
+#endif
 	if (chdir(path) == 0)
 	{
 		return true;
diff --git a/src/common/filesystem.c b/src/common/filesystem.c
index 1cc28f6..c4fd077 100644
--- a/src/common/filesystem.c
+++ b/src/common/filesystem.c
@@ -2281,7 +2281,15 @@ FS_InitFilesystem(void)
 	}
 	else if (strlen(datadir) == 0)
 	{
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+		// realpath() calls getcwd() for a relative path, and the PS5's
+		// kernel has no __getcwd (syscall 326): the process dies with
+		// SIGSYS. The game data sits next to the client in the package,
+		// so the client's folder is the game data's folder.
+		Q_strlcpy(datadir, Sys_GetBinaryDir(), sizeof(datadir));
+#else
 		strcpy(datadir, ".");
+#endif
 	}

 #ifdef _WIN32
